1. Architecture and Working of Riak as a Key-Value NoSQL Database
Riak is a distributed key-value NoSQL database designed for high availability, fault tolerance, and scalability. It follows a masterless architecture based on Amazon’s Dynamo paper.
Architecture
Nodes: All nodes are equal (no single master). Data is distributed across nodes using consistent hashing.
Ring: The keyspace is treated as a circular ring. Keys are hashed and mapped to positions on the ring; each node owns a range of the ring.
Vnodes (virtual nodes): Each physical node hosts multiple vnodes for better load distribution.
Replication: Data is replicated to N nodes (default N=3) for fault tolerance.
Gossip protocol: Nodes exchange membership and ring information.
How Riak stores data
Data is stored as key-value pairs.
A key uniquely identifies a value (any binary object: JSON, text, image, etc.).
Values are stored in buckets. Buckets group related keys and hold configuration (n_val, allow_mult, etc.).
Retrieval is a simple get/put by key → O(1) average time.
