2. Key-Value Data Model in Detail
Structure of data
Simplest NoSQL model: every item is a key that maps to a value.
Key = unique identifier (string, integer, UUID, etc.).
Value = opaque blob (JSON document, binary, text, serialized object).
Optional: buckets / namespaces to group keys.
Key-Value store features
Extremely fast lookups by key.
Simple API: PUT(key, value), GET(key), DELETE(key).
Horizontal scalability via partitioning.
High write and read throughput.
Support for secondary indexes or MapReduce in some systems (Riak).
Data access mechanism
Direct key-based access (hash-based routing).
No complex query language required for basic operations.
Limitations
No rich querying (cannot easily search by value content).
Relationships must be managed in application code.
Limited atomic multi-key operations.
Data modeling requires careful key design.
Why suitable for applications requiring fast lookups
Lookup is O(1) average case.
Ideal for session stores, caching, user profiles by ID, shopping carts by session ID, configuration data, etc.
Minimal overhead compared to relational joins or index scans.
3. Consistency Model of Riak
Riak uses eventual consistency with tunable consistency.
How Riak handles consistency
Uses the N/R/W parameters:
N = number of replicas
R = number of replicas that must respond for a successful read
W = number of replicas that must acknowledge a successful write
Default: N=3, R=2, W=2 → strong enough for most cases while remaining highly available.
Vector clocks (or dotted version vectors) detect concurrent updates (siblings).
Application or last-write-wins (LWW) resolves conflicts.
CAP trade-off
Riak prefers Availability + Partition tolerance (AP) over strong Consistency.
During network partitions, the system remains available; conflicting writes are resolved later (eventual consistency).
Example
Write a user session with W=2, N=3.
Two nodes acknowledge → write succeeds.
A read with R=2 will return the latest value from a majority.
If a partition isolates one replica, the system still serves requests from the other replicas.
4. Transactions and Multi-Operation Transactions in Riak
Traditional relational-style transactions
ACID transactions spanning multiple keys/rows are difficult in distributed key-value stores because:
Data is partitioned across many nodes.
Coordinating a distributed 2-phase commit is expensive and reduces availability.
Network partitions make strong atomicity hard to guarantee.
Possible approaches for maintaining correctness
Single-key atomicity: Riak guarantees atomic read/write of a single key-value pair.
Client-side coordination: Application performs multi-key operations carefully (e.g., using unique request IDs, compensating actions).
Causal consistency / vector clocks: Detect concurrent modifications.
CRDTs (Conflict-free Replicated Data Types): Riak supports CRDTs (counters, sets, maps) that automatically resolve conflicts.
Riak Data Types and strong consistency buckets (in later versions) for limited multi-key needs.
Application-level saga pattern or eventual consistency with reconciliation.
5. Query Features of Riak
Querying by key vs by data
By key: Primary and fastest method (GET /buckets/B/keys/K).
By data: Requires secondary mechanisms because values are opaque.
How Riak supports searching and secondary indexes
Secondary Indexes (2i): Index on integer or binary fields of the value. Allows range queries and exact matches.
Riak Search (or Solr integration in older versions): Full-text search on JSON/XML values.
MapReduce: Distributed processing for complex aggregations (less common now).
Link Walking (legacy): Follow links between objects.
When application requirements become complex
As needs grow beyond simple key lookup (e.g., “find all users in city X”, “search products by name”), secondary indexes or external search engines (Elasticsearch) are added.
Application must maintain indexes or denormalize data into multiple keys.
