8. Relationships in Key-Value Databases
Why relationships are harder
Key-value stores have no built-in foreign keys, joins, or graph traversal.
Relationships must be encoded in the application:
Store related keys inside the value (e.g., list of friend IDs).
Use composite keys or secondary indexes.
Maintain bidirectional references manually.
Suitable techniques
Embedding: Store related data inside the value (good for 1:few).
Reference by key: Store list of related keys.
Secondary indexes: Index on related fields.
Application-level joins: Fetch multiple keys and join in code.
Denormalization: Duplicate data to avoid joins.
Compared with relational (joins) or graph databases (native edges), key-value requires more application logic.
9. Scaling in Riak
How distributed Key-Value databases handle growth
Partitioning (Sharding): Consistent hashing distributes keys evenly across the ring.
Replication: Each key is stored on N nodes → fault tolerance.
Adding nodes: New nodes claim partitions; data is transferred (hinted handoff + anti-entropy).
Read/Write scaling: More nodes = more throughput.
Role of components
Partitioning → horizontal scale-out.
Replication → availability & durability.
Distributed nodes → no single point of failure; load is spread.
Riak automatically rebalances when nodes are added or removed.
10. Operations by Sets in Key-Value Databases
Concept
Many key-value stores (including Riak with CRDTs) support set data types.
Operations: ADD, REMOVE, UNION, INTERSECTION, CONTAINS, SIZE.
Why useful
User groups: Set of user IDs belonging to a group.
Tags: Set of tags for a product or post.
Product categories: Set of product IDs in a category.
Social relationships: Set of followers / following.
Examples
Key group:admins → Set of user IDs.
Key user:123:following → Set of user IDs the user follows.
Adding a tag: SADD product:456:tags "electronics".
Sets allow efficient membership tests and avoid reading the entire list for simple checks.
11. Graph Databases and Their Data Model
Data model
Nodes (vertices): Entities (User, Post, Product…).
Relationships / Edges: Directed or undirected connections between nodes (FRIENDS_WITH, LIKES, FOLLOWS…).
Properties: Key-value pairs on both nodes and relationships.
Labels: Tags on nodes (e.g., :User, :Post) for grouping and indexing.
Why Neo4j is suitable for highly connected data
Relationships are first-class citizens (stored as direct pointers).
Traversals are extremely efficient (index-free adjacency).
Natural fit for social networks, recommendation engines, fraud detection, knowledge graphs, etc.
